# ── Local Firebase project override ─────────────────────────────────
# Copy to `.env.local` (gitignored) and fill in to point THIS machine's dev
# server at a scratch Firebase project instead of the live tournament.
#
# Why you want this: AdminView auto-saves to Firestore on edit, so a dev server
# aimed at production can corrupt a real round with one stray click. With two
# developers, assume it will happen eventually.
#
# Leave every line below unset (or delete .env.local) to use production —
# that's the default and what Vercel builds use.
#
# Values come from the Firebase console: Project settings → General → Your apps
# → SDK setup and configuration. Create a fresh project (e.g. "bourbon-cup-dev"),
# add a Web app, then deploy the shared rules to it:
#
#   firebase use <your-dev-project-id> && firebase deploy --only firestore:rules
#
# All six are required together; setting only some throws at startup.

# VITE_FIREBASE_API_KEY=
# VITE_FIREBASE_AUTH_DOMAIN=
# VITE_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=
# VITE_FIREBASE_STORAGE_BUCKET=
# VITE_FIREBASE_MESSAGING_SENDER_ID=
# VITE_FIREBASE_APP_ID=

# ── Where sign-in is served from ────────────────────────────────────
# OPTIONAL, and unrelated to the six above: this changes the origin that
# hosts Firebase's OAuth handler, not which project the data is in.
#
# By default that origin is <project>.firebaseapp.com. Popup sign-in does not
# care. The REDIRECT flow does — iOS home-screen installs have to use it (see
# src/lib/auth.js), and Safari partitions storage per top-level origin, so a
# handler on a foreign origin can come back from Google with nothing.
#
# Setting this to the app's own domain fixes that. vercel.json already proxies
# /__/auth/* and /__/firebase/* through to the firebaseapp.com handler, so the
# same page is served from our origin. Two things must be true before turning
# it on, or NOBODY can sign in:
#
#   1. The domain is listed under Firebase console → Authentication →
#      Settings → Authorized domains.
#   2. It is a domain the rewrite above actually serves — i.e. production, not
#      a per-branch preview URL.
#
# Set it in the Vercel project (Production only), not here. Unset, sign-in
# works exactly as it does out of the box.

# VITE_AUTH_DOMAIN=thebourboncup.com

# ── Push notifications (FCM web push) ───────────────────────────────
# The PUBLIC half of the Web Push key pair. Safe to commit and safe to ship —
# it goes to the browser either way — but it lives here rather than inline so
# a scratch Firebase project can use its own.
#
# Firebase console: Project settings → Cloud Messaging → Web configuration →
# Web Push certificates → Generate key pair. Copy the "Key pair" value.
#
# Unset, the app behaves normally and no push ever fires: the Notifications
# page reports the key is missing instead of failing quietly. This IS set in
# the Vercel project for production.
#
# Note the service worker (public/firebase-messaging-sw.js) is a static file
# and cannot read env vars — it carries the production Firebase config inline.
# A dev server pointed at a scratch project therefore gets no push at all,
# which is the safe direction to fail.

# VITE_FCM_VAPID_KEY=

# ── Where /api goes during local dev ────────────────────────────────
# `npm run dev` proxies /api to the deployed site, so the GHIN and course
# lookups work locally WITHOUT any credentials on your machine — the deployed
# serverless function holds them. That is the default; you need nothing here.
#
# Set this only to aim somewhere else: a Vercel preview deployment, or a local
# `vercel dev` if you are editing the handlers in api/ themselves (the proxy
# runs the DEPLOYED handler, so local edits to api/ won't show up through it).

# VITE_API_PROXY=https://bourbon-cup-git-my-branch.vercel.app

# ── Serverless API secrets (api/*.js) ───────────────────────────────
# Only needed if you run `vercel dev` — i.e. only if you are changing the
# handlers in api/. Everything that merely CONSUMES them works through the
# proxy above with no secrets at all. In production these are set in the
# Vercel project. Get the values from Aaron via a password manager, and never
# commit them.

# GHIN_EMAIL=
# GHIN_PASSWORD=
# GHIN_COUNTRY=USA
# GOLF_COURSE_API_KEY=
# RAPIDAPI_KEY=

# ── Which edition a FRESH install opens on ──────────────────────────
# Only consulted when a device has no stored pointer; one tap in
# ☰ → Tournaments settles it forever after. Unset, lib/defaultEdition answers
# bc_demo on a store build and bc_2025 on the web — testers and review queues
# are handed the app to try, and the real editions are locked, so landing them
# on a finished cup means a roster they cannot claim.
#
# Set this when the FIELD starts installing the store builds rather than
# twelve testers, or sixteen men will open the app on a demo tournament.
# See docs/store-submission.md §1.4.

# VITE_DEFAULT_EDITION=bc_2026
